lesson: 63
title: Doubles and near doubles
stars: 0
explain: A double is a number added to itself: 7 + 7 = 14.
explain: A near double is a double and one more: 7 + 8 is 7 + 7 and 1 more, so it is 14 + 1 = 15.
explain: When two numbers are next to each other, double the smaller one and add 1.
vars: a=name b=name t=thing n=2..25
answer: n + n + 1
exercise: {a} has {n} {t} and {b} has {n+1} {t}. How many {t} do they have in all?
exercise: On Monday {a} read {n} pages and on Tuesday {n+1} pages. How many pages did {a.he} read in the two days?
exercise: A bus has {n} children on the top deck and {n+1} on the bottom deck. How many children are on the bus?
exercise: {a} scored {n} points in the first game and one more in the second game. How many points did {a.he} score in all?
exercise: There are {n} red {t} and {n+1} blue {t} in a box. How many {t} are in the box?
test: Add {n+1} and {n}. What do you get?
test: {b} found {n+1} {t} and {a} found one fewer than {b}. How many did the two of them find together?
test: A shelf holds {n} books on the left and one more than that on the right. How many books are on the shelf?
exercise: {a} has {n} {t}. {b} has one more than {a}. How many {t} do they have altogether?
exercise: On a farm in Iowa there are {n} cows in one field and {n+1} in the next field. How many cows are in the two fields together?
exercise: What is {n} + {n+1}?
exercise: {a} ate {n} grapes at lunch and {n+1} at tea. How many grapes did {a.he} eat in total?
exercise: {b} put {n+1} {t} in a basket, and {a} put in {n}. How many {t} are in the basket now?
exercise: In Brisbane, {n} people got on the ferry at the first stop and {n+1} people got on at the second stop. Nobody got off. How many people got on in all?
exercise: {a} collected {n.words} {t} on Saturday and one more than that on Sunday. How many {t} did {a.he} collect over the weekend?
exercise: Double {n} and add one. What do you get?
test: Find the sum of {n} and the number just after it.
test: A class in Edinburgh has {n} boys and {n+1} girls. How many pupils are in the class?
test: {b} has {n+1} {t}, which is one more than {a} has. How many {t} do {a} and {b} have between them?
test: What is the total of {n+1} and {n.words}?
test: Over two days a café in Vancouver sold {n} muffins and then one muffin more than on the first day. How many muffins did it sell over both days?
test: {a} has {n} {t}; {b} has just as many plus one. Combined, how many {t} is that?
exercise: got {n} {t}, {b}'s got one more. how many we got together
exercise: {a} read {n} pages mon, {n+1} tues. total?
exercise: {a} scored {n+2} — no wait, {n} — then one more in game 2. how many points altogether
exercise: Kai says {n} + {n+1} is {n+n}. nah, whats the real total
test: {n} kids on top deck, {n+1} downstairs, bus is the number 12 btw. how many kids on it
test: {b} found {n+1} {t}, {a} one less than that. both together how many
test: double {n} plus 1, Rosa reckons thats {n+n+2}?? whats it actually
exam: Whereas {a} is in possession of {n} {t} and {b} is in possession of one item more than {a}, determine the aggregate quantity of {t} held by the two parties.
exam: Compute the sum of {n} and {n+1}.
exam: It is given that {n} pages were read on the first day and {n+1} pages on the second. Ascertain the total number of pages read over the two days.
exam: A quantity of {t} is apportioned such that {a} receives {n} and {b} receives {n+1}. State the total quantity so apportioned.
exercise: When Grandpa passed away, he left {n} dollars to {a} and one dollar more, {n+1}, to {b}. How many dollars did he leave the two of them?
exercise: Since Dad got let go, the family sells pre-loved books: {n} on Saturday and {n+1} on Sunday. How many books did they sell over the weekend?
test: {a} reckons {a.he} ate {n} biscuits — bit economical with the truth there, it was {n+1}. {b} ate {n}. how many did the two of yous scoff, eh
